        rules: {
          objective: 'Capture the most cards from the table. Every card captured is worth 1 point at the end of the game.',
          gameplay: "1. Players take turns playing one card from their hand.\n2. Capturing (Scooping): A card can capture a table card of the same rank (Matching) or multiple table cards that sum to the played card's value (Summing).\n3. Face Cards: K, Q, J have no numerical value and can ONLY capture their own rank.",
          keyRules: [
            'Aces count as 1.',
            'Jokers (if used in amateur games) can scoop the entire table.',
            'Cards not scooped by anyone after the final round go to the player who made the last scoop.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Memory is everything. Track which cards of matching ranks have already been captured so you don't save a card for a match that is impossible.",
          intermediate: "Under Yoruba rules, avoid making 'obvious' sums. If you have a 10, don't leave a 7 and 3 on the table for your opponent; try to scoop them yourself first.",
          advanced: "Esun Management: Build protected piles that require a high card (like a 10) to scoop, ensuring you have that 10 in hand before you start the pile. If an opponent sees your pile building, they may try to 'break' it by scooping components before you finish.",
          tips: [
            "Face cards (K, Q, J) are 'dead weight' if their matches are gone. Dump them early if the table is clear of their rank.",
            'If playing with Isiwo, use it! Checking your pile helps you count the remaining ranks in the deck.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
